# Resolution
Fixed by commit `a81e0245` (`Enforce service-specific SigV4 payload handling`).

The fix makes payload binding a property of the trusted service selected by
endpoint routing, rather than an optional follow-up check owned by individual
HTTP handlers. The commit:

- replaces stringly typed signing-service selection with the exhaustive
  `auth::SigningService::{S3, S3Control, Sts}` enum;
- passes that trusted service through header and presigned SigV4 verification;
- makes STS canonicalize the SHA-256 of the actual request body for both header
  and presigned authentication, regardless of an absent or attacker-supplied
  `x-amz-content-sha256` value;
- therefore rejects explicit `UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD`, altered bodies covered by a
  different fixed hash, and presigned requests whose form body is not bound to
  the signature before any AssumeRole parameters can authorize issuance;
- keeps S3 Control distinct from S3 while implementing its separately
  AWS-pinned payload rules and nested error responses;
- extends the shared AWS/local signing helpers with independently checked AWS
  SDK SigV4 signing for explicit, unsigned, omitted-header, and mismatched-body
  payload modes; and
- updates the authentication fuzz target for the typed provider and signing
  service interfaces.

Permanent shared AWS/local coverage in
`crates/sts-tests/tests/assume_role.rs` proves that header-signed and presigned
AssumeRole requests with a correct explicit body hash succeed, while explicit
`UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD` and fixed-hash body substitution both return the exact AWS
`403 SignatureDoesNotMatch` response without issuing or rendering credentials.
The companion S3 Control matrix in
`crates/s3-tests/tests/s3_control.rs` prevents the STS rule from being
incorrectly generalized: it pins correct hashes, required-header precedence,
AWS-supported unsigned modes, and fixed-hash mismatches. Each accepted S3
Control request writes a distinct tag value and waits for three consecutive
reads of that value; every rejected request verifies that the previous value
remains, so the tests cover mutation as well as response shape.

The AWS-backed tests additionally establish that a missing S3 Control
`x-amz-content-sha256` header wins over both a wrong credential-scope service
and a bad HMAC. This preserves AWS error precedence while closing the STS body
substitution path.

Verification completed for the fix:

- the focused shared STS payload-binding matrix passed against AWS and locally;
- the focused shared S3 Control payload matrix passed against AWS and locally;
- `cargo +nightly fuzz check auth_request` passed;
- Clippy with all targets and features and warnings denied passed;
- formatting checks passed; and
- `cargo nextest run` passed all 7,512 workspace tests with no failures or
  skips.

# Summary
Introduced: the new STS AssumeRole issuance path uses the generic SigV4 authenticator without enforcing payload binding for STS form parameters. The existing S3 path contains a separate payload hash verification step, but the new STS path bypasses it.
The STS handler parses the Query API form body and then calls auth::authenticate_request directly. Unlike the normal S3 service path, it does not perform the follow-up x-amz-content-sha256 payload verification and does not reject PresignedSigV4 or UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD for an operation whose authorization target is in the body. In the auth layer, presigned requests default to UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD when x-amz-content-sha256 is absent, so the signature covers the URL and headers but not the POST form parameters. Because AssumeRole reads RoleArn, RoleSessionName, and DurationSeconds from that unsigned body after authentication, a bearer of a valid presigned STS request can alter the target role/session parameters and receive credentials for any role that the signing principal is otherwise allowed to assume. Similarly, explicit UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD or a mismatched fixed x-amz-content-sha256 header is not rejected by this STS path.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm STS parses Query API form parameters before/independently of authentication and later uses those parameters for credential issuance.
- [x] Confirm presigned SigV4 authentication does not hash or otherwise consume the POST body and defaults to UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD when x-amz-content-sha256 is absent.
- [x] Dynamically prove a presigned STS POST without payload binding accepts attacker-chosen AssumeRole body parameters and issues credentials.
- [x] Dynamically prove a signed, fixed x-amz-content-sha256 header is not recomputed/compared by the STS path by sending a different body and still receiving credentials.
- [x] Compare against the S3 request path to verify the missing STS follow-up payload hash verification is specific to this new path.
## Report
Validated. I added a targeted STS integration-test PoC at crates/sts-tests/tests/presigned_body_tamper.rs and ran it against the real embedded HTTPS test server. The PoC creates a presigned STS POST URL for service sts with signed content-type but no payload binding, then sends attacker-chosen AssumeRole form parameters. It also signs an x-amz-content-sha256 value for an innocent body and sends a different body with that signed hash header unchanged. Command output: `unsigned-payload tamper status=200 contains_tampered=true`; `mismatched-hash tamper status=200 contains_tampered=true`; `test result: ok. 2 passed`. This demonstrates both UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD acceptance and missing fixed-hash verification on STS. Code evidence: crates/server-http/src/http/sts.rs:166-178 parses parameters then calls authenticate_request directly; sts.rs:195-235 later uses RoleArn, RoleSessionName, and DurationSeconds from those parsed body parameters to authorize and issue credentials. crates/auth/src/request.rs:625-630 names the presigned body parameter `_body`, confirming it is unused in presigned auth; request.rs:769-772 defaults absent x-amz-content-sha256 to UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD. The normal S3 path has a separate payload hash comparison at crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:3647-3662, but STS does not call it. Direct crash reproduction is not applicable because this is an authorization/signature-binding bug, not memory corruption; the direct PoC returns 200 rather than crashing. Valgrind and gdb were attempted but unavailable in the container (`command not found`). LLDB was available; a non-interactive breakpoint at sts.rs:221 produced a backtrace through `handle_sts_request_inner` while processing the tampered request body, confirming the vulnerable STS handler path was reached.

